  "text": "Question two. What is a database index, and how does it speed up queries? Find one email among a million users without an index, and Postgres reads all seven thousand three hundred sixty pages of the table to keep one row. An index is a separate structure mapping values to their rows, usually a B-tree, balanced and sorted. This one is three levels deep, so the lookup reads three index pages and one table page: four pages, not seven thousand. That's log n against n, and because the tree is sorted, it answers ranges too. The kind matters. A hash index goes straight to one email, but ask it for a range, and Postgres reads the whole table again.\n\nQuestion three. When does an index help, and when does it hurt? It helps selective reads: equality, ranges, joins, and sorts on the column. It does nothing when most rows match. Half the users are active, and even with an index on that column, Postgres scans the table: one pass beats half a million jumps. And every index costs writes. Ten thousand inserts wrote ten thousand log records with no index, and forty thousand with three, because each insert updates every index too. They take space as well: together, the users table's three indexes are bigger than the table itself. So index the columns you filter and join on, not every column.",
